title: Example - Markdown image - body override
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki;vocab=Markdown

**Pattern:** `![Custom tooltip](image.jpg)` — the user supplies a non-empty body, which becomes the rendered `tooltip` attribute.

[IMAGE marker tiddler](#vocab/markdown/IMAGE) declares `body-attribute: tooltip` AND `attr-tooltip-from: alt-text`. The two interact:

- When source IS a tiddler that has the `alt-text` field set, `attr-tooltip-from` wins — semantic data on the image is authoritative.
- When source is a URL OR the field is empty/absent, `body-attribute` takes over and the user-supplied body becomes the tooltip.

So body-override only takes effect when there's no source-tiddler field to default from. DOM `alt` always comes from `attr-alt-from` when available, regardless of body — alt is for accessibility, not for tooltips, and it should describe the image, not the callsite.

**Live example — external URL, body becomes tooltip:**

![A favicon belonging to TiddlyWiki](https://tiddlywiki.com/favicon.ico)

Hover the image; you should see the body text as the tooltip.

**Live example — source tiddler has alt-text, body is ignored for tooltip:**

![Decorative caption that won't show](#Motovun Jack.jpg)

Hover the image; the tooltip is the source tiddler's `alt-text` field value, not the body.

**Source:**

```
![A favicon belonging to TiddlyWiki](https://tiddlywiki.com/favicon.ico)

![Decorative caption that won't show](#Motovun Jack.jpg)
```

**Why:** priority resolution at parse time:

1. Apply `body-attribute` if body is non-empty → tooltip = body.
2. Check `attr-tooltip-from`: does the source resolve to a tiddler with the named field?
   - Yes → override tooltip with indirect reference to the field. Body discarded.
   - No → leave the body-attribute value in place.

For **external-URL targets** the source-tiddler lookup misses, so body wins.

For **tiddler-title targets without the field**, body also wins (fallback path).

For **tiddler-title targets with the field**, the field wins.

